  "question": "If $X$ is a random variable with $P(X = x) = \\dfrac{k}{2^{x}}, x = 1,2,3,4,$ and $0$ otherwise, find $k$ and $P(X\\geq 3)$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "$\\sum P(X = x) = 1$ for $x=1$ to $4$:<br />$k/2^1 + k/2^2 + k/2^3 + k/2^4 = 1$<br /><br />$= k(1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + 1/16) = k(8/16 + 4/16 + 2/16 + 1/16) = k(15/16)=1$<br /><br />So, $k = 16/15$<br /><br />Now, $P(X\\geq 3) = P(X=3) + P(X=4)$<br />$= k/8 + k/16 = (16/15)\\times(1/8+1/16)= (16/15)\\times(3/16)=3/15=1/5$<br /><br />Final answers: $k=\\dfrac{16}{15}$, $P(X\\geq 3)=\\dfrac{1}{5}$.",
